Q1. What is chunking in RAG?

Chunking is the process of splitting large documents into smaller text segments so that relevant information can be retrieved efficiently.

Q2. What is chunk overlap?

Chunk overlap is the repeated text shared between consecutive chunks. It helps preserve context when information spans chunk boundaries.

Q3. What happens if chunk size is too large?

Chunks may contain unrelated information, reducing retrieval precision and increasing the amount of context passed to the LLM.

Q4. What happens if chunk size is too small?

Important context may be separated, making it harder for the retriever to find complete answers.

Q5. Is chunk size measured in tokens or characters in our implementation?

Our RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter is configured using characters, not tokens.
